    "answer": {
      "short": "Both methods are correct. Mary’s method is simpler; Bob adds the products of terms whose powers sum to $4$.",
      "worked": "Both methods are correct.\n\nMary uses the difference of two squares:\n$$\\begin{align*}(1-x)^6(1+x)^6&=[(1-x)(1+x)]^6\\\\(1-x)^6(1+x)^6&=(1-x^2)^6.\\end{align*}$$\nThe $x^4$ term comes from choosing $-x^2$ twice, so its coefficient is $\\binom{6}{2}(-1)^2=15$.\n\nBob multiplies terms whose powers sum to $4$ and adds their coefficients. This includes every contribution to the same $x^4$ term, so it also gives $15$, with longer working.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
